<!--
    Settings overlay for motorola arcfox.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- arcfox: show the LineageOS peak refresh rate list in Display settings
         (PeakRefreshRateListPreferenceController). It writes
         Settings.System.peak_refresh_rate, the same key stock Settings writes
         for its High/Standard choice, and offers every rate the panel reports
         (60, 90, 120, 165). The default comes from config_defaultPeakRefreshRate
         in the framework overlay (120, as stock). The minimum refresh rate list
         stays hidden on purpose: a minimum equal to the peak pins the panel and
         stops it idling down to 24 Hz. -->
    <bool name="config_show_peak_refresh_rate_switch">true</bool>

    <!-- arcfox: the Motorola face HAL opens the front camera itself during
         enrollment (SensorProps.halControlsPreview) and renders the preview
         into the Surface Settings hands it. Settings must not open the camera,
         or the HAL cannot get it and enrollment runs blind. The HAL's buffers
         are protected (gralloc usage 0x10024900), so the preview has to be a
         SurfaceView; LineageOS change 507847 makes the HAL-owned preview one.
         The pinned buffer size stays at the default face_preview_width/height
         of 1080x1080, an exact entry of camera 1's stream configurations and
         the size verified on this device with the 23.2 Settings fork. -->
    <bool name="config_face_enroll_hal_owns_camera">true</bool>

    <!-- arcfox: Display size per screen. The panels have different default densities
         (inner 420 dpi, cover 360 dpi), so one absolute density set on both, the AOSP
         behaviour, scaled them unevenly: "Large" set while open took the cover from
         360 to 460 dpi. Each panel now keeps its own size; WindowManager stores it by
         the panel's unique id. -->
    <bool name="config_display_size_per_display">true</bool>

    <!-- arcfox: the same idea upstream (LineageOS 396c9de7de9, 2026-09-16): the Display size
         slider changes only the default display and takes its min/max from that display.
         The fork's flag above still covers what upstream does not: the accessibility
         "Display size and text" page and the Display settings summary. -->
    <bool name="config_independent_display_dpi_setting">true</bool>
</resources>
